      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Bolton's granite quarries supplied high‑quality schist for tombstones, feeding artisans like Gershom Bartlett and Jonathan Loomis in the 18th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolton%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1781, Rochambeau’s troops camped at what is now Bolton Heritage Farm, a site on the Washington‑Rochambeau Revolutionary Route.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolton%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s historic library began in 1881 as the Bolton Library Association, later moving to Deacon Charles Loomis’s home before becoming a free public library in 1914.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        17,
        18,
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.boltonct.gov/about",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Bolton hosts an annual Heritage Day, offering self‑guided tours of 18th‑ and 19th‑century homes and mystery photo hunts to celebrate its rich past.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://boltoncthistory.org/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Bolton was once the hunting ground of the Podunk Indians before European settlement, reflecting its early Native American presence.",
